+# Implement JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7386)
+
+## Request
+
+Some of our clients send partial updates in the simpler JSON Merge Patch format. Implement JSON Merge
+Patch, as specified in RFC 7386.
+
+Export a function `applyMergePatch(target, patch)` from `src/index.ts`. It applies a merge patch to a
+target JSON document and returns the merged document.
+
+## Acceptance
+
+- The algorithm is RFC 7386, section 2: when the patch is an object, each member of the patch is applied
+  to the target. A member whose value is `null` removes the member of that name from the target, if it
+  has one. Any other member sets the target member to the merge of its value with the target's member,
+  recursively. Arrays and other non-object values in the patch replace the target value as a whole.
+- When the target is not an object, it is treated as an empty object before the members are applied.
+- When the patch is not an object (a primitive, an array or `null`), the result is the patch itself.
+- The input is not modified: the result is a new value that shares no structure with the input.
+- A patch that is not a JSON value (such as `undefined`, `NaN` or a `Date`) throws a `SyntaxError`,
+  consistent with `applyPatch`.
+- The RFC 7386 appendix A examples give the expected results.
+
+## Scope
+
+- Library code in `src/index.ts`, no runtime dependency. Reuse the copy and JSON-value helpers that
+  `applyPatch` already uses.
+- Tests with `node --test` in `test/merge-patch.test.ts`: the happy path of each rule, the RFC 7386
+  appendix A examples, the error path for a non-JSON patch, and that the input is not mutated.
+- `README.md` documents the new function under a "JSON Merge Patch" section.
+
+## Outcome
+
+- `applyMergePatch` is exported from `src/index.ts`. It follows RFC 7386, section 2: an object patch is
+  merged recursively, a `null` member removes its target member, and any other patch value (array,
+  string, number, boolean) replaces the target value whole. A non-object target is treated as `{}` when the
+  patch is an object; a non-object patch replaces the document.
+- The patch is checked up front with the `isJsonValue` helper that `applyPatch` uses, so a non-JSON patch
+  throws a `SyntaxError`. The result is built from fresh objects, and every value it keeps from the target
+  is copied, so neither input is mutated and the result shares no structure with them.
+- Members are set with `setMember`, so a `__proto__` key stays a plain member.
+- Tests: `test/merge-patch.test.ts` (written first and seen failing with the export missing). It covers
+  the RFC 7386 appendix A examples, each rule, the error path, and non-mutation. `npm test` passes all 98
+  tests. The source type-checks under `tsc --strict` (checked with a temporary TypeScript install).
+- README documents the new function under "JSON Merge Patch".
